  <meta property="og:description" content="130+ free sports games, from a Soccer Career sim and Club Manager to daily trivia and quizzes for the NFL, NBA, MLB, NHL, F1, UFC and more. No login needed.">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="130+ free sports games, from a Soccer Career sim and Club Manager to daily trivia and quizzes for the NFL, NBA, MLB, NHL, F1, UFC and more. No login needed.">
  <!-- Round 314: the moment before React mounts used to show this document's
       full crawler copy as a wall of raw text (the owner filmed it). The block
       stays completely in the DOM for crawlers and for no-JS visitors (the
       noscript lifts the cap), but a browser that is about to boot the app
       sees one dimmed screenful, which reads as the site loading rather than
       a broken page. React replaces #root's children on mount, so the capped
       wrapper lives exactly as long as the wait does. -->
  <!-- Round 448: the dim was not enough. His words, with a screenshot: "that
       thing still shows when u log in into douknowball with the balck screen
       and all that text and i dont want taht". Every sign in with Google
       comes back through a full reload of this document, so the wait before
       React mounts is the first thing a player sees after choosing an
       account, and a screenful of grey paragraphs is what it showed. The
       copy stays exactly where it is for crawlers and for no-JS visitors; a
       booting browser now sees the mark on the app's own background and
       nothing else, because #dukb-boot sits over the copy inside #root and
       React replaces #root's children on mount, so it vanishes at the exact
       moment the app appears. The cap on the copy stays so the document
       cannot scroll during the wait. -->
  <style>
